{"from": "passage:jn-15-01", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture"}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-02", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture"}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-04", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture"}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-05", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture"}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-06", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture"}
{"from": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "observation:grape-fig-cultivation-levant", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture", "grade": "C", "note": "Wine 'has been produced in the Land of Israel since biblical times'; grapes are a staple of ancient Israelite agriculture - the vine cultivation the passage's imagery presupposes. Context only: it shows the custom, never that the metaphor was spoken."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-207-213-temple-gold", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture", "grade": "C", "note": "War 5.207-213 describes the sanctuary gate 'all over covered with gold... it had also golden vines above it, from which clusters of grapes hung as tall as a man's height' - the vine as a familiar and sacred symbol in Jesus' Jerusalem. Context only; it shows the currency of the vine image, not the saying."}
{"from": "observation:grape-fig-cultivation-levant", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-israeli-wine"}
{"from": "observation:grape-fig-cultivation-levant", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-ancient-cuisine"}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-207-213-temple-gold", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-war-5-temple-gold"}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture", "grade": "C", "note": "Herod Archelaus's Judean prutot (4 BCE-6 CE) depict a bunch of grapes: grape-growing was the standard iconography of the Judean economy in the period (reuse from Matthew 02 and 20)."}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-herodian-coinage-archelaus"}
{"from": "text:isaiah-5-1-2", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-isaiah-5-1-2"}
{"from": "text:psalm-69-4", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-psalm-69-4"}
{"from": "claim:jn-15-01-viticulture", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:isaiah-5-1-2", "note": "Biblical cross-reference: Isaiah 5:1-2 is the classic 'vineyard of the LORD' image (a beloved with a vineyard, planted, hedged, with a winepress) behind the vine metaphor; cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-25", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:psalm-69-4", "note": "Biblical cross-reference: 15:25 quotes 'their law' - 'They hated me without a cause' - Psalm 69:4 (Psalm 35:19 says the same); the Psalm is the quoted text, not evidence for the hatred."}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-20", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-10-24", "note": "The same saying: Matthew 10:24 'a servant above his lord' - John adds the persecution application ('If they persecuted me, they will also persecute you'), which Matthew carries at 10:25."}
{"from": "passage:jn-15-21", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-10-22", "note": "The same persecution-for-the-name saying: Matthew 10:22 'You will be hated by all men for my name's sake' - John 15:21 'all these things will they do to you for my name's sake', adding 'because they don't know him who sent me'."}
